48:## Usage caps
49-
50-Every chat turn (one user message, or one approval resume, and everything the agent does to answer it: all model steps, tool calls and delegated sub-agent calls) is limited per user and per agent by a **cost cap** (default $0.25) and a **token cap** (default 300,000 tokens). Each user sets their own caps per agent; they are stored in `data/auth.db` (`agent_caps`) and persist across sessions and restarts. The caps of the agent being chatted with apply, including to the sub-agents it delegates to.
51-
52-- `GET /api/agents/:agentId/caps` returns `{ costCapUsd, tokenCap, defaults }`; `PUT` with `{ costCapUsd, tokenCap }` saves them (both must be greater than 0; at most 1000 USD and 100,000,000 tokens); `DELETE` resets to the defaults. Signed-in users only, own caps only.
53-- Cost is estimated from `src/usage/pricing.ts` (DeepSeek's published peak prices, every input token at the cache-miss rate; unknown models use a high fallback price).
54-- A processor on every agent (`src/usage/turn-budget.ts`) adds up each model step's usage. Once a cap is exceeded the run stops before the next model call (tools the last step requested still run); the partial reply is kept and a closing note explains which limit was hit.
55-- The chat stream carries a `data-turn-usage` part (`{ costUsd, tokens, inputTokens, outputTokens, steps, capCostUsd, capTokens, capped, reason, final }`) after each model step and once more at the end. The final usage (plus the turn's Neatlogs `traceId`) is also on the finish part and on the saved reply as `metadata.turnUsage`. Neatlogs traces get `app.turn_cost_usd`, `app.turn_tokens`, `app.turn_steps` and `app.turn_capped`, plus a `cost-capped` or `token-capped` tag.
56-- After a turn, Neatlogs' own numbers are shown next to the estimate under the bot pill (e.g. "Est. $0.0022 · 7.3k" / "Neatlogs $0.0001 · 7.3k tokens"). Once the reply is sent and the trace has ended, the server flushes the Neatlogs exporter and polls `get_trace_context` for that trace (server-side, through the shared Neatlogs MCP client; never exposed to an agent) until it is finalized, for up to ~45 s (`src/usage/neatlogs-usage.ts`). In practice it is ready about 4-6 s after the reply. The result is saved on the reply as `metadata.neatlogsUsage` (`{ status: "pending" | "ready" | "unavailable", traceId, costUsd, tokens, inputTokens, outputTokens, checkedAt, fetchedAt }`), so a reload shows it. The browser polls `GET /api/chat/:agentId/messages/:messageId/neatlogs-usage`, which only finds replies in the caller's own chat (404 otherwise) and, when no poll is running and the last check is over 10 s old, checks Neatlogs once more. Neatlogs' cost is usually lower than the estimate because it counts DeepSeek cache hits; the caps still use the estimate.
57-
58-## Key Integrations
59-
60-- **NeatLogs**: All Mastra agents are wrapped with `neatlogs/mastra`, recording agent runs, model steps, tool calls, inputs/outputs, timing, token usage when returned, and errors. CLI runs have request traces. Tracing does not trim model context.
61-- **GitHub MCP**: The GitHub Bot uses GitHub's hosted MCP server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) through `@mastra/mcp`, limited to the context, repos, issues, pull_requests and actions toolsets.
62-- **Neatlogs MCP**: The Neat Bot uses Neatlogs' hosted MCP server (`https://ingest.neatlogs.com/mcp`) through `@mastra/mcp`, authenticated with the existing `NEATLOGS_API_KEY`. Tools are gated by name (the server sends no read-only annotations): reads run directly, writes and unknown tools need approval.
63-- **Entire**: Coding-context hooks and checkpoint settings are configured.
